digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56798-7
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper aims to develop and evaluate Pb0.92Ba0.08[Zr0.50+xTi0.48−x(Nb0.5Sb0.5)0.02]O3 piezoceramics by combining precise Zr/Ti-ratio tuning at the morphotropic phase boundary with tape casting, lamination, and hot pressing, in order to test whether a flattened energy landscape and a densified, low-defect microstructure can overcome the piezoelectricity-temperature-stability trade-off.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When device requirements expose a piezoelectricity-temperature-stability trade-off, and both intrinsic phase-boundary mechanisms and extrinsic processing mechanisms are identified as actionable routes, a coherent research aim is to develop and evaluate an integrated ceramic design that tests those routes together."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) Because devices require both excellent piezoelectricity and good temperature stability, and piezoceramics face an unresolved trade-off between these properties, the study is motivated to devise a strategy that alleviates this trade-off and delivers both properties."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Previously used instability-introducing strategies that inevitably decrease Curie temperature are likely to limit the workable temperature range and thereby undermine usable temperature stability."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) In random PZT-based ceramics, pores and oxygen vacancies generated during high-temperature sintering and Pb volatilization plausibly contribute to inferior poling and domain switching because such defects hinder saturated poling and domain switching."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) The PBZTNS-x study is justified in centering on a synergistic design that combines MPB-based phase-boundary engineering with defect- and porosity-suppressing process engineering to improve piezoelectricity and temperature stability."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Since most prior work used only one process technique with limited enhancement, while the reported three-technique combination is rare and improves density while reducing PbO volatilization, this combination motivates evaluating multi-technique processing for this specific PBZTNS ceramic strategy rather than claiming a universal superiority of all multi-technique approaches."];
  "n10_s" [label="(10,0,0) Currently, in textured piezoceramics, high texturing degree and large grain size enable more sufficient domain switching and more saturated poling, yielding stable phase structure and domain configurations."];
  "n12_s" [label="(12,0,0) Currently, random PZT-based ceramics generate many pores and oxygen vacancies due to high sintering temperature and Pb volatilization."];
  "n13_s" [label="(13,0,0) Currently, pores lower density and breakdown strength; oxygen vacancies create dipole defects; both hinder saturated poling and domain switching."];
  "n15_s" [label="(15,0,0) Currently, if pores are eliminated and oxygen vacancies reduced in PZT ceramics, then both high piezoelectricity and improved temperature stability are anticipated."];
  "n16_s" [label="(16,0,0) Currently, a new strategy is desired that is more economical and practical than texturing."];
  "n17_s" [label="(17,0,0) Currently, a synergistic design is proposed: combine phase boundary engineering + process engineering to boost piezoelectricity and temperature stability (validated in PBZTNS-x)."];
  "n19_s" [label="(19,0,0) Currently, at MPB, flattened Landau energy profile and nanoscale multi-phase coexistence enhance dielectric response, consequently boosting piezoelectricity."];
  "n22_s" [label="(22,0,0) Currently, approaching MPB lowers Landau energy barrier between ferroelectric phases, promoting dielectric and piezoelectric properties."];
  "n25_s" [label="(25,0,0) Currently, process techniques (tape casting/lamination/hot isostatic pressing) are applied to the MPB-featured composition selected by Strategy 1."];
  "n31_s" [label="(31,0,0) Currently, most prior literature used only one process technique and obtained limited enhancement."];
  "n33_s" [label="(33,0,0) Currently, tape casting can improve microstructure and is widely used for ultra-thin, multilayer, and textured ceramics fabrication."];
  "n34_s" [label="(34,0,0) Currently, tape casting alone hardly improves piezoelectricity and temperature stability simultaneously for a single composition without templates."];
  "n35_s" [label="(35,0,0) Currently, based on Strategy 1, combining three process techniques significantly improves density and reduces PbO volatilization; this combination was rarely reported."];
  "n3_s" [label="(3,0,0) Currently, devices (sensors/actuators) require both excellent piezoelectricity and good temperature stability to ensure sensitivity and reliable outputs."];
  "n5_know" [label="(0,0,0) If an application requires two properties simultaneously and existing materials show an unresolved trade-off, then a central research objective is to devise a strategy that alleviates the trade-off and delivers both properties."];
  "n6_s" [label="(6,0,0) Currently, the trade-off stems from different contributions (mechanistic origins) of the two properties."];
  "n8_know" [label="(0,0,0) If a strategy decreases Tc (which dictates workable temperature range), then it tends to undermine temperature stability/usable temperature window for devices that need stable operation across temperature."];
  "n8_s" [label="(8,0,0) Currently, previously used instability-introducing strategies (donor doping/relaxor end members/local heterogeneity) inevitably decrease Curie temperature Tc, limiting the workable temperature range."];
  "n9_s" [label="(9,0,0) Currently, the texturing method can enhance both piezoelectricity and temperature stability without increasing Tc, but it is complex and costly."];
  "n_combo_rationale" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given that phase-boundary engineering can raise intrinsic piezoelectricity and that process engineering can reduce defects/porosity to improve temperature stability (and also help poling and piezoelectricity), therefore combining phase boundary engineering with process engineering is a promising general strategy to alleviate the trade-off."];
  "n_econ_need" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given that a more economical/practical strategy than texturing is desired and that texturing is complex and costly, therefore the chosen strategy should avoid relying on texturing-like complexity/cost while still enabling simultaneous property improvement."];
  "n_extrinsic_fix" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given that random PZT ceramics have pores/oxygen vacancies from processing and that pores/vacancy-related dipole defects hinder saturated poling and worsen both properties, therefore reducing pores and oxygen vacancies is a key extrinsic route to improve both piezoelectricity and temperature stability."];
  "n_phase_need" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given that MPB-related enhanced dielectric response boosts piezoelectricity and that approaching MPB lowers the Landau barrier promoting dielectric/piezoelectric properties, therefore phase-boundary (MPB) engineering should be used to raise intrinsic piezoelectricity."];
  "n_proc_need" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given that eliminating pores/reducing oxygen vacancies is expected to improve both properties and that pores/oxygen-vacancy dipole defects hinder saturated poling/domain switching, therefore process engineering that densifies and suppresses vacancies should improve both piezoelectricity and temperature stability."];
  "n_process_evidence" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given that process techniques are applied to the MPB-featured composition and that process engineering is expected to reduce pores/oxygen vacancies and improve both properties, therefore the observed pore/vacancy reduction and property gains are best explained by effective process-engineering-driven densification and defect suppression on the MPB-engineered composition."];
  "n_tape_cast_limit" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given that tape casting is widely used and can improve microstructure but that tape casting alone hardly improves both properties simultaneously without templates, therefore tape casting should be combined with additional processes (e.g., lamination/hot pressing) rather than used alone to target simultaneous improvement."];
  "n_text_clue" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given that texturing improves both properties without increasing Tc and that high texturing degree/large grain size enable saturated poling and stable phase/domain configurations, therefore a plausible key cause for simultaneous improvement is achieving saturated poling/domain switching and stable phase/domain configurations rather than raising Tc."];
  "n9_s" -> "n_text_clue" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
  "n10_s" -> "n_text_clue" [label="abduction-knowledge"];
  "n15_s" -> "n_proc_need" [label="deduction-case"];
  "n13_s" -> "n_proc_need" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "n19_s" -> "n_phase_need" [label="deduction-case"];
  "n22_s" -> "n_phase_need" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "n_phase_need" -> "n_combo_rationale" [label="induction-case"];
  "n_proc_need" -> "n_combo_rationale" [label="induction-common"];
